# TSHARK
Nixpkgs: wireshark
Source: https://www.wireshark.org
Description: Terminal-based network protocol analyzer (CLI version of Wireshark)
Version: 4.6.4

## Commands

### Basics
- tshark -D: list available network interfaces
- tshark -i [interface]: capture packets on a specific interface
- tshark -r [file].pcap: read packets from a pcap file

### Essentials
- tshark -f "tcp port 80": apply a capture filter (BPF syntax) before capturing
- tshark -Y "http.request": apply a display filter (Wireshark syntax) during or after capture
- tshark -w [file].pcap: write captured packets to a file

### Intermediaries
- tshark -T fields -e frame.time -e ip.src -e ip.dst: extract and display specific fields
- tshark -n: disable network object name resolution (faster, prevents DNS traffic)
- tshark -z io,phs: display protocol hierarchy statistics

### Advanceds
- tshark -i [interface] -b duration:3600 -w [filename]: capture using a ring buffer (new file every hour)
- tshark -r [file].pcap -2 -R "tcp.analysis.retransmission": two-pass analysis for complex session filters

### Uncommons
- tshark -G protocols: dump the internal protocol database
- tshark -D -L: list link-layer types for a specific interface

## Tips
- use -n to avoid slow DNS lookups during live captures
- capture filters (-f) are more efficient for high-volume traffic, while display filters (-Y) are more flexible for analysis
- pipes work well: tshark -r file.pcap -T json | jq

## Disclaimers
- capturing network traffic usually requires administrative privileges (sudo or root)
- respect privacy laws and organizational policies regarding network monitoring

## Alternatives
- tcpdump: the classic, lower-level packet sniffer
- termshark: a TUI (Terminal User Interface) wrapper for tshark that feels like Wireshark

